  <content>
  <section>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the following lines given in slope-intercept form.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(y=3x +4\) </item>
  <item> \(y=3x - 2\) </item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
  
    <para> Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/763bf9e9b6700dc554c82e9addacab94c734ce91"/></media></figure></para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the relationship between the two lines you graphed in question 1?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>They are parallel lines.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>When you examine the equations of the lines, what do you notice is the same about the equations? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
    
    <list>
      <item>The slopes of the lines are equal.</item>
      <item>The numbers in front of the variables are the same.</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>When you examine the equations of the lines, what do you notice is different about the equations? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
    
    <list>
      <item>The constant term is different in each equation.</item>
      <item>The number that is added to the equation is different. </item>
      <item>The \(y\)-intercepts for each equation is different.</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="bfcc2540-a04e-4cd5-9e68-013f6be9f291" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para>When you examine the graph of the lines, how many vertical spaces are between the two lines?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! There are 6 vertical spaces between the two lines.</para><para>Try again. Think about how the lines are spaced on the graph.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Interaction End -->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool above or technology outside the course, on your coordinate grid, use the slope and \(y\)-intercept of each equation to graph each equation. Use a different color for each equation.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(y=3x-2\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-\frac13 x-3\) </item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-top="4" data-bottom="-4" data-left="-15" data-right="15" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
  
    <para> Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6b0a2dd1c0662d5beda17d819a3671482baf7e41"/></media></figure></para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>When you examine the graph of the lines in question 6, explain if the lines appear to be perpendicular. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <list>
      <item>No, the lines don't look like they intersect at right angles - so they don't look like they are perpendicular. (Check the settings of your graph, this is incorrect.)</item>
      <item>Yes, the lines look like they intersect at right angles - so they appear perpendicular. (This is the correct answer.)</item>
    </list>
    
    <para>
      Sometimes lines that are perpendicular do not appear to intersect at right angles because of the settings of the graph. To more easily identify perpendicular lines, change the settings of your Desmos graph. 
      In your graph, select the “wrench tool” icon.
      In the grid section of the menu that pops up, select the “Zoom Square” option.
    </para>
    
    <para>Remember to identify perpendicular lines algebraically, examine their slopes. If the slope values are opposite reciprocals, then the lines intersect at right angles and are perpendicular. </para>
  
  </solution></exercise></section>
</content>
